{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Elasticsearch?

**Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics engine that turns JSON documents
into structures optimized for finding text, filtering records, and aggregating values.**
It is built on Apache Lucene and exposes its indexing and search operations through
network APIs.

An application sends documents to an **index**, Elasticsearch routes each document to
one **primary shard**, and Lucene builds searchable structures inside that shard.
Queries fan out to the relevant shard copies and a coordinating node merges their
partial results.

{% callout variant="info" %}
The core invariant is that every accepted document belongs to one primary shard, and
search can only return the version represented by a refreshed searchable segment.
Replicas improve availability and read capacity; they do not replace an independent
backup or source of truth.
{% /callout %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Index documents" eyebrow="Write model" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Map each field deliberately, analyze searchable text into terms, and route one document
to one primary shard.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Retrieve evidence" eyebrow="Read model" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Run full-text queries, exact filters, ranges, and aggregations against the field
structures created at index time.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Operate copies" eyebrow="Distributed model" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Place primary and replica shard copies across nodes, monitor recovery, and retain enough
capacity for growth and failure.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Build the mental model from document to shard

Elasticsearch names several layers that are easy to confuse:

- **Document:** one JSON record with a stable `_id` and fields such as `title`, `price`,
  or `created_at`.
- **Index:** a logical collection with one mapping and a configured number of primary
  shards.
- **Primary shard:** the authoritative Lucene index for one partition of the documents.
- **Replica shard:** another copy of one primary shard that can serve reads and be
  promoted after a primary failure.
- **Segment:** an immutable Lucene search structure created as indexed changes become
  searchable; later merges combine smaller segments.
- **Node:** one Elasticsearch process that may coordinate requests, hold shard copies,
  or perform other cluster roles.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Document ownership" value="1 primary" detail="Routing chooses exactly one primary shard for each document" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Physical copies" value="1 + replicas" detail="Copies must be allocated on different nodes" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Search shape" value="Scatter + gather" detail="Shard results are merged by a coordinating node" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric label="Visibility" value="After refresh" detail="Durable acceptance and search visibility are different moments" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The primary-shard count is part of an index generation's physical design. You can add
replicas later, but changing partitioning usually means creating another index through
split, shrink, rollover, or reindex workflows.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Trace indexing and search as different paths

### Indexing path

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Route the document" label="Coordinator" %}
Resolve the write alias, validate the request, and hash the routing value to one primary
shard.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Apply on the primary" label="Write authority" %}
Update Lucene and the shard's recovery history, then forward the operation to assigned
replica copies.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Acknowledge the write" label="Durability contract" %}
Return according to the request's active-copy and timeout contract. A timeout does not
by itself prove whether the write committed.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Refresh for search" label="Near real time" %}
Publish a searchable segment so later searches can see the indexed version. Refresh is
not the same operation as a durable flush or snapshot.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Search path

{% system-flow title="Distributed query path" caption="Every participating shard performs local retrieval; the coordinator owns the final result contract." %}
{% system-node title="Client query" eyebrow="Intent" icon="search" tone="blue" %}
Send full-text clauses, exact filters, sorting, aggregations, a result limit, and a
deadline.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Coordinating node" eyebrow="Scatter" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Choose one eligible copy of each target shard and send the shard-level request in
parallel.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Lucene shard copies" eyebrow="Local work" icon="layers" tone="cyan" %}
Match terms and filters, compute local scores or buckets, and return a bounded candidate
set.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Merged response" eyebrow="Gather" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Combine global top results or aggregation states, hydrate requested fields, and expose
timeout or partial-result behavior.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

Fan-out means one user query can create work on many shards. Measure shard requests,
candidate counts, and tail latency, not only top-level request rate.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Map each field for the questions it must answer

A mapping defines field types and the structures Elasticsearch builds. Field type is a
query contract, not cosmetic schema documentation.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="text" eyebrow="Relevance" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Analyze human language into terms for `match` and phrase queries. A plain `text` field is
not the default choice for sorting or ordinary terms aggregations.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="keyword" eyebrow="Exact value" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
Keep a structured value as one term for filters, grouping, and sorting. Normalizers can
standardize exact values without tokenizing them into words.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Multi-field" eyebrow="Two access paths" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Index one source string as `text` for relevance and as a `keyword` subfield for exact
facets or ordering.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Numeric and date" eyebrow="Typed range" icon="gauge" tone="amber" %}
Use typed values for range queries, date math, sorting, and numeric aggregations instead
of encoding them as strings.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Dynamic mapping is convenient for exploration, but uncontrolled field names can expand
cluster state and produce accidental types. Production indexes should use explicit
mappings, bounded templates, or a deliberate `flattened` representation for arbitrary
key sets.

{% interactive-block id="technology/elasticsearch-mapping-query-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/data/mapping-query-model.json" /%}

When an existing field has the wrong type or analyzer, plan a new index generation and
reindex. Treat a mapping choice as hard to reverse even when a narrow parameter happens
to be updatable.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Size shards from recovery and workload constraints

Shard planning balances two opposing risks:

- **Too many small shards:** more cluster metadata, per-shard memory, segment overhead,
  and fan-out work.
- **Too few large shards:** less parallelism, larger failure units, and longer relocation
  or recovery.
- **Too few nodes for copies:** replicas remain unassigned because a primary and its
  replica cannot occupy the same node.
- **Too little disk headroom:** allocation watermarks and recovery copies can prevent
  the cluster from restoring redundancy.

Current Elastic guidance gives roughly 10-50 GB and fewer than 200 million documents as
useful starting points for many workloads, not universal guarantees. Benchmark real
documents, mappings, queries, storage, merge pressure, and recovery on production-like
hardware.

{% interactive-block id="technology/elasticsearch-shard-capacity-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/data/shard-capacity-model.json" /%}

### Capacity equations

- `future primary bytes = current primary bytes + daily growth * planning days`
- `average primary shard bytes = future primary bytes / primary shard count`
- `stored bytes = future primary bytes * (1 + replicas) * measured index overhead`
- `average bytes per data node = stored bytes / data nodes`

These averages reveal impossible plans, but they do not expose hot routing keys,
uneven time-series rollover, merge bursts, or concurrent recovery. Keep measured
headroom beyond the arithmetic minimum.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Treat Elasticsearch as a derived read model

For application search, the transactional database or event log usually remains the
system of record. Elasticsearch is a replaceable projection designed around retrieval.

{% system-flow title="Replayable search indexing" caption="A durable change stream lets operators rebuild search without scraping an inconsistent live database." %}
{% system-node title="System of record" eyebrow="Commit" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Persist the authoritative entity and a durable outbox event in one consistency boundary.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Change stream" eyebrow="Order" icon="workflow" tone="violet" %}
Retain stable entity IDs, source versions, deletes, and enough history to replay a new
index generation.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Index writer" eyebrow="Project" icon="repeat" tone="amber" %}
Transform the event, reject stale versions, and index by stable ID so a replay replaces
the same logical document.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Read alias" eyebrow="Serve" icon="route" tone="green" %}
Point search traffic at one validated generation and move the alias atomically after
backfill and catch-up complete.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Reindex without mixing contracts

1. Create `products-v2` with the new mapping, analyzer, shard plan, and lifecycle policy.
2. Record a source checkpoint and backfill every document through the same transformer
   used for live events.
3. Catch up mutations after that checkpoint and compare counts, sampled documents,
   search relevance, latency, and error rate.
4. Move the read and write aliases only after the new generation satisfies explicit
   release gates.
5. Keep rollback and independent snapshots until the old generation's retention window
   expires.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/code/reindex-alias-cutover.py" language="python" title="Gate an alias cutover on checkpoint and validation evidence" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Design for node loss and degraded clusters

Cluster health summarizes shard assignment:

- **Green:** every primary and replica shard copy is assigned.
- **Yellow:** all primary shards are assigned, but at least one replica is unassigned;
  current data is searchable, but redundancy is reduced.
- **Red:** at least one primary shard is unassigned, so some index data is unavailable.

### Failure rules

- A replica can be promoted after primary failure only if an in-sync copy exists and is
  allocated on another node.
- Adding replicas cannot protect a cluster that has too few eligible nodes or zones to
  place those copies.
- Recovery and relocation consume disk, network, CPU, and I/O alongside live queries and
  indexing.
- A replica repeats accidental deletes and corrupt application writes; restore requires
  independent snapshots or replayable source data.
- Cross-zone placement improves fault isolation, but surviving nodes still need capacity
  for the redistributed workload.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Never respond to a red cluster by deleting data or forcing stale shard copies without
first identifying which primary is missing, what good copies or snapshots exist, and
what data loss the action would accept.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Write queries that bound work

Use full-text queries for relevance and filter context for exact eligibility. Keep
tenant and authorization constraints in non-scoring filters so they cannot be bypassed
by ranking behavior.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/code/products-index-template.json" language="json" title="Explicit product mapping with text and keyword access paths" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/code/product-search-request.json" language="json" title="Bounded search with relevance, filters, facets, and deterministic sort" /%}

### Query guardrails

- Cap result size and use stable continuation such as `search_after` for deep paging;
  do not make large offsets the routine pagination path.
- Limit expensive aggregations by time range, tenant, cardinality, and bucket count.
- Return only fields the client needs instead of moving entire documents by default.
- Set deadlines and decide whether timed-out or partial results are acceptable for each
  product surface.
- Separate search relevance experiments from authorization and availability rules.
- Capture slow-query evidence with query shape, index generation, shard count, and
  filter selectivity.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Secure and operate the cluster

### Security boundary

- Require authenticated encrypted connections for clients and node communication.
- Grant least-privilege index, document, and field access through controlled service
  identities; do not expose cluster administration to application callers.
- Prevent untrusted users from supplying arbitrary query DSL, scripts, index names, or
  unbounded aggregation shapes.
- Separate tenants physically when noisy-neighbor risk, compliance, or deletion proof
  cannot be handled safely by shared filters.
- Audit administrative changes, access failures, snapshot operations, and alias moves.

### Query-plane signals

- End-to-end and per-shard latency, timeout rate, rejected searches, and concurrent
  search pressure.
- Search fan-out, cache behavior, result count, aggregation bucket count, and slow-query
  traces.
- Relevance quality on a stable judged query set, not only infrastructure latency.

### Data-plane signals

- Indexing rate, rejected writes, refresh and merge pressure, segment count, and version
  conflicts.
- Primary and replica assignment, relocation progress, recovery speed, and allocation
  explanations.
- Disk watermarks, heap and garbage collection, filesystem cache pressure, and node
  saturation.
- Snapshot success, restore duration, replay lag, lifecycle rollover, and alias state.

{% interactive-checklist checklistId="elasticsearch-production-readiness" dataFile="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/data/production-readiness.json" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Choose Elasticsearch for the retrieval problem it solves

### Strong fit

- Full-text relevance over large document collections.
- Faceted search combining text, exact filters, ranges, and aggregations.
- Operational exploration of logs or events with explicit lifecycle controls.
- A derived search projection that can tolerate bounded indexing lag.

### Reconsider or pair it with another store

- Multi-row transactional invariants and normalized joins belong in a transactional
  database.
- Direct object storage remains better for durable large binary payloads.
- A tiny exact lookup workload may not justify a distributed search cluster.
- Strict read-after-write product paths need a source lookup, checkpoint wait, or bounded
  overlay instead of pretending refresh lag cannot occur.
- Long-term recovery needs snapshots or source replay, not replica copies alone.

The design question is not "Can Elasticsearch store this JSON?" It is "Which retrieval
contract deserves a distributed search index, and how will we rebuild it when the
mapping, shard plan, or source data changes?"
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Test the production decisions

The assessment checks mapping behavior, distributed request paths, shard arithmetic,
failure states, and safe reindex operations.

{% quiz questionsFile="/api/content/technology/elasticsearch/quiz/elasticsearch-production.json" lessonSlug="elasticsearch" title="Elasticsearch Production Quiz" /%}
{% /section-card %}
